<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue sinusoidal wave profile traveling to the right. An orange boat sits on the profile at x sub b of t. A selected green crest is marked at x sub c of t. A gold double arrow between two neighboring crests marks the wavelength lambda.">
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-profile" text="The idealized sinusoidal water surface at one instant."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-travel" text="A right-pointing arrow showing the direction in which the wave pattern travels."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-lambda" text="The horizontal separation lambda between two neighboring crests."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-boat" text="The boat at its horizontal position x sub b of t."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-boat-left" text="The thick left edge completes the outline of the boat's hull."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-selected-crest" text="The chosen crest at horizontal position x sub c of t."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-amplitude" text="The vertical amplitude A from mean water level to crest height."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Suppose the boat travels with constant velocity <m>v</m>, while every
crest travels to the right with constant speed <m>k&gt;0</m>:
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-positions" number="yes">
<mrow>x_b(t)\amp=vt,</mrow>
<mrow>x_c(t)\amp=kt.</mrow>
</md>
At <m>t=0</m>, the boat and the selected crest are both at the origin.
A positive <m>v</m> means the boat travels with the waves, and a
negative <m>v</m> means it travels against them.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-dependency-tree">
<caption>
The chain-rule dependencies for
<m>w=f(x_b(t),x_c(t))</m>. Multiplying along the two paths from
<m>w</m> to <m>t</m> and adding gives the total rate of change.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-dependency-tree">
<diagram dimensions="(330,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.35,-0.55,2.35,3.55)">
<line at="dop-tree-w-xb" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.45,1.5))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-tree-w-xc" endpoints="((0,3),(1.45,1.5))"
      stroke="#0072B2" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-tree-xb-t" endpoints="((-1.45,1.5),(0,0))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-tree-xc-t" endpoints="((1.45,1.5),(0,0))"
      stroke="#0072B2" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dop-tree-w" p="(0,3)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="dop-tree-xb" p="(-1.45,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dop-tree-xc" p="(1.45,1.5)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="dop-tree-t" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>

<label at="dop-tree-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.13)" alignment="n"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>w=f(x_b,x_c)</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xb-lab" anchor="(-1.58,1.5)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>x_b</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xc-lab" anchor="(1.58,1.5)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>x_c</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.13)" alignment="s">
<m>t</m>
</label>

<label at="dop-tree-w-xb-lab" anchor="(-0.95,2.48)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-w-xc-lab" anchor="(0.95,2.48)" alignment="e"
       color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xb-t-lab" anchor="(-0.95,0.55)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>\frac{dx_b}{dt}=v</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xc-t-lab" anchor="(0.95,0.55)" alignment="e"
       color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dx_c}{dt}=k</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A diamond-shaped dependency tree. The water height w equals f of x sub b and x sub c at the top. Orange and green intermediate nodes x sub b and x sub c lie in the middle, and time t lies at the bottom. The branches carry the two partial derivatives and the velocities v and k.">
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-w-xb" text="The branch from w to the boat position carries partial f with respect to x sub b."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-xb-t" text="The branch from the boat position to time carries d x sub b over d t, equal to v."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-w-xc" text="The branch from w to the selected crest position carries partial f with respect to x sub c."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-xc-t" text="The branch from the selected crest position to time carries d x sub c over d t, equal to k."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-t" text="Both paths end at the independent variable t."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-video">
<caption>
The moving-wave model, its chain-rule tree, the rate calculation, three
boat-speed comparisons, nonzero cancellation when the boat keeps pace
with the waves, and the encounter-period formula.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cr-doppler"
       source="videos/chain_rule_doppler_effect_manim.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/chain_rule_doppler_effect_manim.jpg"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-rate">
<statement>
<p>
Find the rate of change of <m>w</m> with respect to <m>t</m> using the
chain rule.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let
<m>\theta=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x_b-x_c)</m>. The two partial
derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}
\amp=-\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}\sin\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}
\amp=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}\sin\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
Evaluating at <m>(x_b(t),x_c(t))</m> and following the two paths in
<xref ref="fig-cr-doppler-dependency-tree"/> gives
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-rate" number="yes">
<mrow>
\frac{dw}{dt}
\amp=f_{x_b}x_b'+f_{x_c}x_c'
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}\sin\theta\,(-v+k)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}(k-v)\sin\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
Using <m>x_b-x_c=(v-k)t</m>, define
<m>\theta(t)=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(v-k)t</m>. Then
<md>
\frac{dw}{dt}=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}(k-v)\sin\theta(t).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-constant-height">
<statement>
<p>
How can the water-surface height at the boat stay at zero (the mean water
level) for all time, even though the waves are moving?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The boat must keep pace with the waves. If <m>v\ne k</m>, the distance
<m>x_b-x_c</m> keeps changing, so the height at the boat runs through
crests and troughs. If <m>v=k</m>, then <m>x_b-x_c</m> is constant, so
the height is constant, and it is zero exactly when the boat sits a
quarter wavelength from a crest, where the cosine in the model
vanishes. For example, choose
<md>
<mrow>x_b(t)\amp=kt-\frac{\lambda}{4},</mrow>
<mrow>x_c(t)\amp=kt.</mrow>
</md>
The phase is <m>-\pi/2</m>, so <m>w(t)=A\cos(-\pi/2)=0</m> for all
<m>t</m>, and since <m>\sin\theta=-1</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}\frac{dx_b}{dt}
\amp=\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}\frac{dx_c}{dt}
\amp=-\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-nonzero-cancellation">
<caption>
Nonzero cancellation when <m>v=k</m>. With
<m>x_b(t)=kt-\lambda/4</m> and <m>x_c(t)=kt</m>, the boat remains on a
sloping, zero-height part of the wave. The boat-motion and crest-motion
terms have equal magnitude and opposite signs.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-nonzero-cancellation">
<diagram dimensions="(510,275)" margins="14">
<definition>cancelwave(s) = cos(pi*(s-4)/4)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.65,-2.25,9.25,2.15)">
<line at="dop-cancel-mean" endpoints="((0,0),(8.8,0))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<graph at="dop-cancel-wave" function="cancelwave" domain="(0,8.8)"
       stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dop-cancel-boat-point" p="(2,0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dop-cancel-crest-point" p="(4,1)" fill="#009E73"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-crest-guide" endpoints="((4,0),(4,1))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>

<polygon at="dop-cancel-boat"
         points="((1.55,0.12),(2.45,0.12),(2.27,-0.18),(1.73,-0.18))"
         fill="#D55E00" fill-opacity="0.30" stroke="#D55E00"
         thickness="2"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-boat-left" endpoints="((1.55,0.12),(1.73,-0.18))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-boat-mast" endpoints="((2,0.12),(2,0.52))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-boat-lab" anchor="(1.82,0.33)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>x_b=kt-\frac{\lambda}{4}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-cancel-crest-lab" anchor="(4.1,1.1)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>x_c=kt</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-cancel-quarter" endpoints="((2,-1.72),(4,-1.72))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-quarter-left" endpoints="((2,-1.55),(2,-1.86))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="1"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-quarter-right" endpoints="((4,-1.55),(4,-1.86))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="1"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-quarter-lab" anchor="(3,-1.65)" alignment="n"
       color="#E69F00">
<m>\lambda/4</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-cancel-boat-term" endpoints="((1.72,0.22),(1.72,1.22))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-boat-term-lab" anchor="(1.55,1.38)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>f_{x_b}x_b'=\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda}</m>
</label>
<line at="dop-cancel-crest-term" endpoints="((2.28,-0.22),(2.28,-1.22))"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-crest-term-lab" anchor="(4.65,-1.3)" alignment="c"
       color="#0072B2">
<m>f_{x_c}x_c'=-\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-cancel-sum-lab" anchor="(7.25,1.45)" alignment="c"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\frac{dw}{dt}=0,\qquad w(t)=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue sinusoidal snapshot with a green crest at x sub c equals k t and an orange boat a quarter wavelength to its left at x sub b equals k t minus lambda over four. At the boat, an orange upward arrow and a blue downward arrow represent equal nonzero chain-rule contributions that cancel.">
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-point" text="The boat samples the mean-height crossing one quarter-wavelength behind the selected crest."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-left" text="The thick left edge completes the outline of the boat's hull."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-mast" text="The mast identifies the orange hull as the boat."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-crest-point" text="The selected crest at x sub c equals k t."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-quarter" text="The fixed horizontal separation lambda over four."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-term" text="The positive boat-motion contribution, two pi A k over lambda."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-crest-term" text="The negative crest-motion contribution, negative two pi A k over lambda."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-sum-lab" text="The two contributions sum to zero, and the measured height remains zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The equal, nonzero terms cancel, so <m>dw/dt=0</m>. A zero derivative
at one isolated crest or trough need not mean <m>v=k</m>; matching
speeds is what keeps the height constant over time. Any other fixed
offset holds some other constant height between <m>-A</m> and
<m>A</m>. At a tracked crest, for instance, <m>w(t)=A</m>, but there
both chain-rule terms are <m>0</m> and nothing cancels.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-period">
<statement>
<p>
How does the boat's velocity affect the time between successive crests?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Substituting the position functions into the model gives
<md>
w(t)=A\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(v-k)t\right).
</md>
For <m>v\ne k</m>, one complete cycle occurs when the cosine's argument
changes by <m>2\pi</m>. Therefore the time between successive crests is
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-period" number="yes">
T=\frac{\lambda}{|k-v|},
\qquad v\ne k.
</md>
Equivalently, the encounter frequency is <m>|k-v|/\lambda</m>. When
<m>v=k</m>, the sampled height is constant, so it has no finite
fundamental period.
</p>

<p>
For example, suppose adjacent crests are <m>8</m> meters apart and
travel at <m>2</m> meters per second.
</p>

<table xml:id="table-cr-doppler-periods">
<title>
Time between crests for <m>\lambda=8\text{ m}</m> and
<m>k=2\text{ m/s}</m>
</title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell>Boat's motion</cell>
<cell><m>v</m> (m/s)</cell>
<cell>Time between crests</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Stationary</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m> seconds</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Traveling with the waves</cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m> seconds</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Traveling against the waves</cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>8/3</m> seconds</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-speed-comparison">
<caption>
Normalized height <m>w/A</m> over eight seconds for the three velocities
in <xref ref="table-cr-doppler-periods"/>. Traveling against the waves
produces three cycles, remaining stationary produces two, and traveling
with the waves produces one.
</caption>
<image width="94%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-speed-comparison">
<diagram dimensions="(520,360)" margins="14">
<definition>against(t) = 3 + 0.72*cos(3*pi*t/4)</definition>
<definition>stationary(t) = 0.72*cos(pi*t/2)</definition>
<definition>withwaves(t) = -3 + 0.72*cos(pi*t/4)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-5.25,9.6,4.25)">
<line at="dop-speed-top-base" endpoints="((0,3),(8,3))"
      stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dop-speed-mid-base" endpoints="((0,0),(8,0))"
      stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dop-speed-bot-base" endpoints="((0,-3),(8,-3))"
      stroke="#B3B3B3" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dop-speed-time-axis" endpoints="((0,-4.85),(8.45,-4.85))"
