<label at="dop-speed-period-against-lab" anchor="(1.333,1.65)"
       alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>T=\frac{8}{3}</m>
</label>
<line at="dop-speed-period-stationary"
      endpoints="((0,-1.44),(4,-1.44))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<label at="dop-speed-period-stationary-lab" anchor="(2,-1.35)"
       alignment="n" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>T=4</m>
</label>
<line at="dop-speed-period-with"
      endpoints="((0,-4.44),(8,-4.44))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<label at="dop-speed-period-with-lab" anchor="(4,-4.35)"
       alignment="n" color="#009E73">
<m>T=8</m>
</label>

<label at="dop-speed-against-lab" anchor="(8.18,3.45)" alignment="e"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>v=-1</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-speed-stationary-lab" anchor="(8.18,0.45)" alignment="e"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>v=0</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-speed-with-lab" anchor="(8.18,-2.55)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>v=1</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three stacked normalized height traces over eight seconds. The top orange trace for v equals negative one completes three cycles and has period eight thirds. The middle dark red trace for v equals zero completes two cycles and has period four. The bottom green trace for v equals one completes one cycle and has period eight.">
  <annotation ref="dop-speed-against" text="The height trace for a boat traveling against the waves at one meter per second; it completes three cycles."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-speed-stationary" text="The height trace for a stationary boat; it completes two cycles."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-speed-with" text="The height trace for a boat traveling with the waves at one meter per second; it completes one cycle."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-speed-period-against" text="One period of length eight thirds seconds on the top trace."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-speed-period-stationary" text="One period of length four seconds on the middle trace."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-speed-period-with" text="One period of length eight seconds on the bottom trace."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-measure-wave">
<statement>
<p>
<em>Can the boat measure the wave speed and wavelength?</em>
Suppose <m>k</m> and <m>\lambda</m> are unknown. A stationary boat
encounters crests every <m>4</m> seconds. Traveling with the waves at
<m>1\text{ m/s}</m>, it encounters them every <m>8</m> seconds.
Assuming the crests move faster than the boat, determine <m>k</m> and
<m>\lambda</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Using meters and seconds, the period formula gives
</p>
<md>
<mrow>4\amp=\frac{\lambda}{k},</mrow>
<mrow>8\amp=\frac{\lambda}{k-1}.</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Here <m>k-1&gt;0</m> by assumption. Thus
</p>
<md>
<mrow>\lambda\amp=4k=8(k-1),</mrow>
<mrow>k\amp=2,\qquad \lambda=8.</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Therefore the wave speed is <m>2\text{ m/s}</m> and the wavelength is
<m>8\text{ m}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-varying-amplitude">
<statement>
<p>
<em>What if the waves grow taller while the boat keeps pace with a
crest?</em> Let <m>A=A(t)</m>, with <m>\lambda</m> fixed. Must the
measured height remain constant? What additional chain-rule term is
needed?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Regard the height as the three-variable function
</p>
<md>
<mrow>w\amp=f(A,x_b,x_c)</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=A\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x_b-x_c)\right).
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
The chain rule now includes the amplitude path:
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{dw}{dt}
\amp=\frac{\partial f}{\partial A}\frac{dA}{dt}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad+\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}\frac{dx_b}{dt}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad+\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}\frac{dx_c}{dt}.
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Writing <m>\phi=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x_b-x_c)</m>, this becomes
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{dw}{dt}\amp=A'(t)\cos\phi
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad+\frac{2\pi A(t)}{\lambda}(k-v)\sin\phi.
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
At a tracked crest, <m>\phi</m> is a multiple of <m>2\pi</m> and
<m>v=k</m>. Hence
</p>
<md>
\frac{dw}{dt}=A'(t).
</md>
<p>
The measured height is therefore constant only when <m>A'(t)=0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-both-changing">
<statement>
<p>
<em>What if both the relative position and amplitude are changing?</em>
Let <m>\lambda=8\text{ m}</m> and, for <m>t\ge 0</m>, let
</p>
<md>
<mrow>A(t)\amp=(1\text{ m/s})t,</mrow>
<mrow>x_b(t)\amp=(1\text{ m/s})t,</mrow>
<mrow>x_c(t)\amp=(2\text{ m/s})t.</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Thus <m>v=1\text{ m/s}</m> and <m>k=2\text{ m/s}</m>. At
<m>t=1\text{ s}</m>, find <m>dw/dt</m>. Identify the contribution from
the changing amplitude, then express the position contribution both as
one relative-position term and as separate boat and crest terms.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Set <m>r=x_b-x_c</m> and
<m>\phi=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}r</m>. The three-variable chain rule gives
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{dw}{dt}
\amp=f_AA'
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad+f_{x_b}x_b'
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad+f_{x_c}x_c'.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Combining only the two position paths, write
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
C_r
\amp=f_{x_b}x_b'+f_{x_c}x_c'
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=-\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}r'\sin\phi.
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Therefore
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{dw}{dt}
\amp=A'\cos\phi+C_r
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=A'\cos\phi
-\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}r'\sin\phi.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
At <m>t=1\text{ s}</m>,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
A(1)\amp=1\text{ m},
\qquad A'(1)=1\text{ m/s},
</mrow>
<mrow>
r(1)\amp=-1\text{ m},
\qquad r'(1)=-1\text{ m/s},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\phi(1)\amp=-\frac{\pi}{4}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The amplitude contribution is
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
C_A
\amp=A'(1)\cos\phi(1)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\text{ m/s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\approx0.707\text{ m/s}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The combined relative-position contribution is
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
C_r
\amp=-\frac{2\pi A(1)}{\lambda}
r'(1)\sin\phi(1)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=-\frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{8}\text{ m/s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\approx-0.555\text{ m/s}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Equivalently, keeping the boat and crest paths separate gives
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
f_{x_b}v
\amp=\frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{8}\text{ m/s},
</mrow>
<mrow>
f_{x_c}k
\amp=-\frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{4}\text{ m/s}.
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
These two terms have sum
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
f_{x_b}v+f_{x_c}k
\amp=-\frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{8}\text{ m/s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=C_r,
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
which agrees with the combined relative-position calculation. Finally,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
w'(1)
\amp=C_A+C_r
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=\frac{\sqrt{2}(4-\pi)}{8}\text{ m/s}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\approx0.152\text{ m/s}.
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
The positive net rate means that the growing amplitude outweighs the
decrease caused by the boat moving away from the selected crest at this
instant.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<conclusion>
<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-extensions-video">
<caption>
Using two encounter periods to recover the wave speed and wavelength,
then extending the chain rule when the wave amplitude varies with time.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cr-doppler-extensions"
       source="videos/chain_rule_doppler_extensions_manim.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/chain_rule_doppler_extensions_manim.jpg"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
<p>
<em>Conceptual takeaway.</em> The two chain-rule terms separate the
change caused by the boat's motion from the change caused by the moving
wave pattern. When the boat moves with the waves, those contributions
oppose one another; when it moves against the waves, they reinforce one
another. They can partly cancel or cancel completely, and the remaining
relative speed <m>|k-v|</m> determines how frequently the boat encounters
crests.
</p>
<p>
For more physical context, see
<url href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/17-4-doppler-effect-and-sonic-booms">OpenStax, <em>Doppler Effect and Sonic Booms</em></url>.
</p>
</conclusion>
</activity>

</subsection>

</section>
